[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# EST in Lamassu (/docs/pr-preview/pr-78/platform/iot-fleets/est/configuration)\n\n\n\nEach DMS exposes the EST operations its devices use to obtain and renew their identity. This page describes the routes, how each operation authenticates, the DMS settings that govern each endpoint and request examples with curl and OpenSSL. To create and manage the DMS, see the [DMS guide](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms); to prepare the device's credentials and initial trust, see [Authentication and trust](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity).\n\nEndpoint reference [#endpoint-reference]\n\nEach DMS publishes its operations at `/.well-known/est/{APS}/{operation}`, where `APS` is the DMS ID, not its display name. Gateway configuration determines the public host and route. The router also admits routes without the `APS` segment, but the controller requires it, so do not use them.\n\n| Operation                            | Method | Authentication and condition                                               |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| [`/cacerts`](#cacerts)               | GET    | No EST credential required; HTTPS and infrastructure controls still apply. |\n| [`/simpleenroll`](#simpleenroll)     | POST   | Initial authentication and DMS registration and replacement policy.        |\n| [`/simplereenroll`](#simplereenroll) | POST   | Renewal authentication, existing identity, subject, revocation and window. |\n| [`/serverkeygen`](#serverkeygen)     | POST   | Initial authentication and server-side key generation enabled.             |\n\nLamassu does not implement `/csrattrs` or `/fullcmc`. There is no native JWT EST authentication mode either; a webhook can interpret a request credential according to its own contract.\n\nAuthentication per operation [#authentication-per-operation]\n\nThe DMS authenticates the request before it applies registration and issuance rules. Each operation reads its authentication from its own `est_rfc7030_settings` block:\n\n* `settings.enrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simpleenroll` and `/serverkeygen`.\n* `settings.reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simplereenroll`.\n\nThe two blocks are stored independently and neither inherits from the other, so read both before editing. `auth_mode` accepts these values:\n\n| `auth_mode`                               | Control applied in the configured operation          |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`                      | Presented certificate validation.                    |\n| `EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK`                        | Configured webhook authorization.                    |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_AND_EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK` | Certificate validation followed by webhook approval. |\n| `NO_AUTH`                                 | No DMS requester authentication.                     |\n\nWebhook modes also run during re-enrollment when configured in its block. An absent or unrecognized mode makes the operation fail: there is no implicit downgrade to `NO_AUTH`. `NO_AUTH` does not establish ownership of the requested ID.\n\nClient certificate [#client-certificate]\n\nThe certificate modes read `client_certificate_settings` in the same block:\n\n| Field                           | Description                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `validation_cas`                | IDs of the CAs the presented certificate is validated against. The first one that validates it is used.                         |\n| `allow_expired`                 | **Allow expired certificates**: accepts expired certificates in initial enrollment.                                             |\n| `chain_level_validation`        | When greater than 0, only that many certificates of the presented chain are validated.                                          |\n| `allow_unverifiable_revocation` | **Allow certificates without revocation information**: accepts certificates without revocation information; `false` by default. |\n\nThe validator receives one certificate credential; the flow does not guarantee forwarding every handshake intermediate to it. For a direct case, configure the bootstrap’s direct issuer as validation CA and test the actual Gateway path.\n\n**Revocation.** In initial enrollment the client certificate and every intermediate CA between it and the validation CA are checked, each against its issuer; extra certificates the device sends in the handshake are ignored. Certificates managed by Lamassu are checked against their stored status, and external ones through their OCSP and CRL endpoints. The operation fails closed when revocation cannot be verified: unreachable endpoints, stale or invalid evidence and inconclusive statuses always reject the certificate. A validation CA that is revoked in Lamassu also rejects the operation, without falling back to the remaining validation CAs. [Revocation and checks](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity#revocation-and-checks) details the evidence the endpoints must present.<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> CRL lists fetched for these checks are reused for a limited time before being downloaded again; see </ins>[<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Revocation and checks</ins>](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity#revocation-and-checks)<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> for the caching window and its effect on revocation visibility.</ins>\n\n`allow_unverifiable_revocation` relaxes a single case: a certificate, or an intermediate CA of the chain, that Lamassu does not manage and that lists no OCSP or CRL endpoint. With the option on, that certificate is accepted even if it is revoked, and the console shows the warning **Revocation will not be verified**. The option is set separately in the enrollment and re-enrollment blocks, and it is off in DMSs created before it existed.\n\n**Re-enrollment.** The validator tries the enrollment CA first and then the re-enrollment block's `validation_cas`. That list is optional because the enrollment CA is always trusted, and CAs listed only in the initial block's `validation_cas` do not extend re-enrollment acceptance: during a CA rotation, add the old issuance CA to the re-enrollment block's list. The validator compares only the presented TLS leaf against those CAs and checks only its revocation. In this block `chain_level_validation` and `allow_expired` are stored but not consulted; expiry is governed by [`enable_expired_renewal`](#renewal-with-an-expired-certificate).\n\nExternal webhook settings [#external-webhook-settings]\n\nThe webhook modes read `external_webhook_settings` in the same `est_rfc7030_settings` block. These settings define the destination, method, timeout and how Lamassu authenticates to the receiver; the [webhook contract](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity#webhook-contract) describes the device credential, the request body and the expected response.\n\n| `external_webhook_settings` field | Decision                                                                                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `url`                             | Absolute `http` or `https` URL of the receiver. It must be the final URL; redirects are not followed.                                            |\n| `method`                          | `POST` or `PUT`; `POST` by default.                                                                                                              |\n| `name`                            | Label used in service logs.                                                                                                                      |\n| `config.auth_mode`                | `noauth`, `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls`. An absent or unknown value behaves as `noauth`.                                                             |\n| `config.log_level`                | Log level of the webhook call: `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error`; `info` by default.                                                            |\n| `config.apikey_options`           | Required for `apikey`: `header` and `key`. Reserved header names are rejected.                                                                   |\n| `config.jwt_options`              | Required for `jwt`: `oidc_client_id`, `oidc_client_secret` and an absolute `http` or `https` `oidc_well_known`.                                  |\n| `config.mtls_options`             | Required for `mtls`: `cert_content` and `key_content` as Base64-encoded PEM, like certificates elsewhere in the API; plain PEM is also accepted. |\n| `config.insecure_skip_verify`     | Skips verification of the receiver certificate when `true`. Verification is on by default. Unlike alert channels, this flag is honored here.     |\n| `config.call_timeout`             | Maximum duration of the authorization call (for example `10s`); 10 seconds by default.                                                           |\n\nCreating or updating a DMS that uses the webhook in any of its blocks validates this configuration. When it is invalid, the response is HTTP 400, with a body that starts with `struct validation error:` and continues with `invalid enrollment external webhook config` or `invalid reenrollment external webhook config`. These rules apply:\n\n* The URL, method and options must be valid, and `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content` must decode to a matching certificate and private key.\n* File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are never valid, because the service does not read files. Enter the certificate and key as PEM, Base64-encoded or plain, in `cert_content` and `key_content`.\n* A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or the receiving service trusts (for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded`), and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-` (case-insensitive). The [DMS API reference](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager) documents the `WebhookCall` and `WebhookCallHttpClient` schemas.\n* With `config.auth_mode` set to `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls`, the body must carry its option block (`apikey_options`, `jwt_options` or `mtls_options`) and no other. The legacy `apikey`, `oidc` and `mtls` names fail validation, and `validate_server_cert` is ignored: use `insecure_skip_verify`. Only bodies without option blocks are accepted as `noauth`, and their credentials are dropped.\n* The `http_client` key belongs to alert channels; in a DMS body it is ignored. The DMS configuration goes in `external_webhook_settings.config`.\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Credentials over http://\">\n  With credentials configured, they are sent regardless of the URL scheme: an `http://` webhook receives the API key or bearer token in cleartext, and mTLS has no effect because no TLS handshake takes place, so the client certificate is never presented. Prefer `https://` for `url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises.\n</Callout>\n\nThe console exposes independent editors for the enrollment and re-enrollment authentication, with the same **Log Level**, **Call Timeout**, **HTTP Authentication** and credential fields as the webhook channels, plus a **Skip TLS Verification** switch (`insecure_skip_verify`, off by default; the console warns when you enable it). The editor rejects file paths in the certificate and key fields, and saving rewrites `external_webhook_settings.config` with the current keys and the material Base64-encoded. Configurations migrated from 3.8.0 that referenced files or relied on `validate_server_cert: false` need fixing; see [DMS webhooks](/docs/upgrades/3-8-0-to-4-0-0#dms-webhooks).\n\n<span id=\"compatibility-notes\" />\n\nBefore you begin [#before-you-begin]\n\nFor an end-to-end first enrollment, follow [Enroll and connect your first device](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device).\n\nThe examples on this page need the public HTTPS DMS URL, server trust obtained through an approved channel, the operation's authentication mode and an admitted identifier. They use Bash, curl with `--fail-with-body` and OpenSSL 3.x ([openssl-pkcs7](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-pkcs7/) to extract certificates and [openssl-x509](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-x509/) to prepare renewal); work in a private directory. If you integrate a generic EST client, first check the Lamassu specifics described for each operation, such as the PEM response or the multipart format of `/serverkeygen`.\n\nPrepare `est-trust.pem` to verify the EST server, `bootstrap.crt` and `bootstrap.key` for initial certificate authentication, and `device-root.pem` as the approved root of the device issuer. The two trust files can contain different roots. For another authentication mode, adapt the credentials according to [Authentication and trust](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity). Replace the host and `REPLACE_DMS_ID` with those of your installation:\n\n```bash\nset -euo pipefail\numask 077\nEST_BASE='https://lamassu.example.com/.well-known/est/REPLACE_DMS_ID'\n```\n\nWhen a request fails, retain the HTTP code, headers and body before decoding anything. A 500 does not mean a transient failure: service denials return it too. [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/troubleshooting) explains how to interpret them.\n\n/cacerts [#cacerts] [#cacerts]\n\n`GET /cacerts` retrieves the certificates published by the DMS. The client can inspect the bundle and use approved certificates to build trust chains.\n\nConfiguration [#trust-distribution] [#trust-distribution]\n\n`settings.ca_distribution_settings` determines which certificates `/cacerts` returns:\n\n| Field                                                                                    | Content it adds                                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| <span id=\"lamassu-tls-certificate\" />`include_system_ca` (**Include Lamassu System CA**) | The available downstream TLS certificate. It can be a leaf, not an issuing CA. |\n| <span id=\"enrollment-ca\" />`include_enrollment_ca` (**Include Enrollment CA**)           | The selected enrollment CA certificate.                                        |\n| <span id=\"managed-cas\" />`managed_cas` (**Managed CAs**)                                 | The explicitly selected CA certificates.                                       |\n\n<span id=\"what-cacerts-returns\" />\n\nThe DMS does not add the ancestors of those CAs: selecting the enrollment CA does not publish its root or intermediates, which must be selected in **Managed CAs**. The set may contain leaves, roots and intermediates, including more than one hierarchy during a migration. A certificate's position does not define the chain: identify certificates by subject, issuer, AKI/SKI, signature and constraints.\n\nRequest and response [#cacerts-request-and-response] [#cacerts-request-and-response]\n\nSend an HTTPS GET with an empty body and no EST credential. Gateway controls still apply.\n\n* **Default response.** `Content-Type: application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. The body is Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7 with lines of up to 76 characters.\n* **PEM response.** Lamassu selects this format when `Accept` is exactly `application/x-pem-file` and returns the same value in `Content-Type`. The body contains the certificates in PEM.\n\nExample [#download-and-inspect-cacerts] [#download-and-inspect-cacerts]\n\nThis example downloads the default response and converts it to PEM. It uses `EST_BASE` and `est-trust.pem` from [Before you begin](#before-you-begin).\n\n```bash\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem \\\n  --dump-header cacerts.headers --output cacerts.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/cacerts\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in cacerts.b64 -out cacerts.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in cacerts.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out cacerts.pem\nopenssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile cacerts.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout\n```\n\nCheck fingerprints, issuers and dates against the [distribution configuration](#trust-distribution). Do not turn the entire bundle into trusted roots or use it to authenticate its own initial download. HTTP 200 here does not prove that authentication or issuance works.\n\n/simpleenroll [#simpleenroll] [#simpleenroll]\n\n`POST /simpleenroll` issues a device’s initial certificate from a CSR. Lamassu takes the device identifier from the CN and binds the issued certificate to the device identity. The private key stays on the client.\n\nConfiguration [#enrollment-configuration] [#enrollment-configuration]\n\nThe initial enrollment options are stored in `settings.enrollment_settings`:\n\n| Field                                                                                     | Description                                                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| <span id=\"enrollment-settings\" />`protocol`                                               | `EST_RFC7030` for these operations.                                                                                                  |\n| <span id=\"issuing-ca\" />`enrollment_ca`                                                   | ID of the CA that signs the certificates.                                                                                            |\n| <span id=\"enrollment-device-registration\" />`registration_mode`                           | `JITP` creates an unknown device with the CSR CN as ID and the DMS as owner. `PRE_REGISTRATION` requires the ID to exist beforehand. |\n| <span id=\"device-provisioning-profile\" />`device_provisioning_profile`                    | Tags, metadata and icon a device registered through JITP receives.                                                                   |\n| <span id=\"certificate-replacement\" />`enable_replaceable_enrollment`                      | Allows `/simpleenroll` for an existing ID in the same DMS.                                                                           |\n| <span id=\"csr-signature-verification\" />`verify_csr_signature` (**Verify CSR signature**) | Verifies possession of the CSR key. Keep it `true`.                                                                                  |\n| <span id=\"authentication-mode\" />`est_rfc7030_settings`                                   | Initial enrollment authentication; see [Authentication per operation](#authentication-per-operation).                                |\n\n**Registration and replacement.** With `PRE_REGISTRATION`, an existing device goes through the replacement check first, even without an identity; review the [preregistration conditions](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies#preregistration-and-replacement) before using this mode. Initial enrollment rejects an ID owned by another DMS. Replacement can attempt to revoke the previous certificate even if a later phase fails: it is not atomic. Do not enable it as an automatic response to an error; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/troubleshooting) distinguishes repeated IDs, ownership and partial changes.\n\n<span id=\"issuance-profile\" />\n\n**Issuance profile.** The profile is defined in `settings`, not in `enrollment_settings`, and applies to all three operations that issue certificates. `settings.issuance_profile_id` takes precedence over `settings.issuance_profile`; without either, the CA default profile is used. The resolved profile must define an end-entity certificate: a `Sign as CA` profile is rejected at enrollment and re-enrollment time. The rules are documented in [Profiles](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). DMS changes affect future requests; they do not modify signed certificates or install client trust.\n\nRequest and response [#simpleenroll-request-and-response] [#simpleenroll-request-and-response]\n\nSend `Content-Type: application/pkcs10` with a Base64-encoded DER PKCS#10 CSR in the body. Lamassu decodes the body directly as Base64, with no PEM or JSON wrapper.\n\n* **Default response.** `Content-Type: application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` and `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64`. The PKCS#7 contains the issued certificate. Retrieve the CA certificates you need to validate its chain separately.\n* **PEM response.** Send exactly `Accept: application/x-pem-file` to receive the certificate in PEM with `Content-Type: application/x-pem-file`.\n\nExample [#initial-enrollment-example] [#initial-enrollment-example]\n\nThe example uses initial `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE` authentication and the files from [Before you begin](#before-you-begin).\n\n<span id=\"prepare-the-csr\" />\n\n1. **Prepare the CSR.** The example generates a new EC P-256 key and uses `est-device-001`, which must be available if the DMS registers automatically. The `Common Name` becomes the device identifier. Check that the profile permits the selected key and subject.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e device.key\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out device.key\nopenssl req -new -sha256 -key device.key -subj '/CN=est-device-001' -out device.csr\nopenssl req -in device.csr -verify -noout\nopenssl req -in device.csr -outform DER -out device.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in device.csr.der -out device.csr.b64\n```\n\nAn implementation using hardware or its own store must produce the CSR through its resident key. Honor Extensions does not authorize every requested attribute: see [Profiles](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles).\n\n<span id=\"request-the-identity\" />\n\n2. **Request the identity.** Send the CSR, authenticating with the bootstrap credential:\n\n```bash\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert bootstrap.crt --key bootstrap.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @device.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header enroll.headers --output enroll.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/simpleenroll\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in enroll.b64 -out enroll.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in enroll.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out device.crt\n```\n\nIf curl fails, the example stops before decoding: an error response is not a certificate. A timeout leaves the operation's result to be checked in Lamassu, so do not automatically repeat issuance.\n\n<span id=\"check-and-install-the-result\" />\n\n3. **Check and install the result.** For a root that issues directly:\n\n```bash\nopenssl verify -CAfile device-root.pem -purpose sslclient device.crt\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -noout -subject -issuer -serial -dates -text\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -pubkey -noout > certificate-public.pem\nopenssl pkey -pubin -in certificate-public.pem -outform DER -out certificate-public.der\nopenssl pkey -in device.key -pubout -outform DER -out key-public.der\ncmp certificate-public.der key-public.der\n```\n\n`cmp` must exit with code 0. Also verify that the CN is correct, the certificate is a leaf, its uses permit your application and its dates satisfy the profile. With intermediates, provide the chain through `-untrusted` as described in [PKI validation](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation).\n\nQuery the device in Lamassu: the owning DMS, current serial number and history must match the result. Protect the key/certificate pair and install both using the device's mechanism; association in Lamassu does not install files in the client. Check a real connection before considering the process complete.\n\n<span id=\"next-steps\" />\n\n/simplereenroll [#simplereenroll] [#simplereenroll]\n\n`POST /simplereenroll` issues a successor certificate for an active identity. The client keeps the device identifier and can request the certificate with a new key.\n\nConfiguration [#re-enrollment-configuration] [#re-enrollment-configuration]\n\nThe renewal options are stored in `settings.reenrollment_settings`:\n\n| Field                                                                                                 | Description                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| <span id=\"renewal-authentication\" />`est_rfc7030_settings`                                            | Re-enrollment authentication, independent of the initial one; see [Authentication per operation](#authentication-per-operation).                              |\n| <span id=\"renewal-validation-cas\" />`est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas` | Re-enrollment validation CAs, tried after the enrollment CA. Optional; typically populated during a CA rotation while devices with old-CA certificates renew. |\n| <span id=\"renewal-window\" />`reenrollment_delta`                                                      | The renewal window opens at the active certificate’s `NotAfter` minus this delta.                                                                             |\n| <span id=\"preventive-threshold\" />`preventive_delta`                                                  | Preventive monitoring threshold. Produces states and notifications; it does not extend the window.                                                            |\n| <span id=\"critical-threshold\" />`critical_delta`                                                      | Critical monitoring threshold. Produces states and notifications; it does not extend the window.                                                              |\n| <span id=\"renewal-with-an-expired-certificate\" />`enable_expired_renewal` (**Allow expired renewal**) | Accepts an expired presented certificate in the renewal mTLS validator. Does not authorize another mode or remove Gateway constraints by itself.              |\n| <span id=\"previous-certificate-revocation\" />`revoke_on_reenrollment` (**Revoke on Re-enrollment**)   | If the previous certificate is `ACTIVE`, attempts to revoke it with reason Superseded before binding its successor.                                           |\n\nThe legacy flat field `reenrollment_settings.additional_validation_cas` is not part of the API: it is ignored without error, so an update body built with it empties `validation_cas`. Read the DMS before editing it through the API.\n\n<span id=\"continuous-operation\" />\n\nMonitoring does not renew: the client must schedule the request and installation with time for disconnections, trust distribution and consumer checks. Stagger requests across the population and bound retries within the window; authentication, subject and policy errors require correcting their cause. Maintain an admitted and tested recovery mechanism for lost keys and pairs that could not be installed; see [Recover according to state](#recover-according-to-state).\n\nRequest and response [#simplereenroll-request-and-response] [#simplereenroll-request-and-response]\n\nUse the same [CSR format and headers](#simpleenroll-request-and-response) as `simpleenroll`. The response contains the successor certificate in Base64-encoded DER PKCS#7, or in PEM if you send exactly `Accept: application/x-pem-file`.\n\nWith certificate authentication, Lamassu requires the credential’s CN to match the CSR’s CN and compares the requested subject with the current certificate. The DMS rejects a revoked current certificate or a request made before the renewal window. [Client certificate](#client-certificate) details accepted CAs, expiry and revocation checks.\n\nExample [#renewal-example] [#renewal-example]\n\nThe example continues the [initial enrollment](#initial-enrollment-example) in the same Bash session, with `device.crt` and `device.key` as the current identity.\n\n<span id=\"prepare-renewal\" />\n\n1. **Prepare renewal.** Before requesting the successor:\n   * Query the current certificate in Lamassu and compare it with the installed certificate. You need its subject, serial and dates, as well as the client key.\n   * Check the re-enrollment authentication mode: it is independent of initial authentication. With `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`, use the current identity; do not assume the bootstrap credential is admitted.\n   * Calculate the window's start: the current certificate's `NotAfter` minus `reenrollment_delta`.\n   * Review **Revoke on Re-enrollment**. When enabled, Lamassu can revoke the previous identity before the client installs the new one.\n\n<span id=\"request-the-successor-certificate\" />\n\n2. **Request the successor certificate.** The request authenticates with `device.crt` and `device.key`, and the CSR contains a new key compatible with the profile. Conversion from the certificate preserves the subject and copies only SAN when present; check the subject and requested names before sending it. The backend checks the subject against the current identity: it attempts RawSubject equality and then compares CN, OU, O, L, ST and C, so rebuilding only the CN can lose required attributes.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e device.next.key\ntest ! -e device.next.crt\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out device.next.key\nopenssl x509 -x509toreq -in device.crt -key device.next.key -sha256 \\\n  -copy_extensions copy -ext subjectAltName -out device-renewal.csr\nopenssl req -in device-renewal.csr -verify -noout -subject\nopenssl req -in device-renewal.csr -outform DER -out device-renewal.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in device-renewal.csr.der -out device-renewal.csr.b64\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert device.crt --key device.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @device-renewal.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header renewal.headers --output renewal.b64 \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/simplereenroll\"\nopenssl base64 -d -in renewal.b64 -out renewal.p7b\nopenssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in renewal.p7b -print_certs -quiet -out device.next.crt\nopenssl verify -CAfile device-root.pem -purpose sslclient device.next.crt\nopenssl x509 -in device.next.crt -noout -subject -issuer -serial -dates\nopenssl x509 -in device.next.crt -pubkey -noout > next-certificate-public.pem\nopenssl pkey -pubin -in next-certificate-public.pem -outform DER -out next-certificate-public.der\nopenssl pkey -in device.next.key -pubout -outform DER -out next-key-public.der\ncmp next-certificate-public.der next-key-public.der\n```\n\nAlso check extensions, dates and that the serial matches the current slot in Lamassu. Do not resubmit a failed request without querying that state: issuance, previous revocation and successor binding can have partial results.\n\n<span id=\"install-and-check-the-new-pair\" />\n\n3. **Install and check the new pair.** Keep the result as `device.next.crt` and `device.next.key` until verified. Switch the pair through a coordinated operation in the device store or configuration; avoid temporarily exposing a certificate with another version's key.\n\nTest mTLS with the consumer and verify that it uses the new serial. Confirm the history and current certificate in Lamassu. If **Revoke on Re-enrollment** was disabled, the previous identity can remain valid: retire it according to PKI policy after testing the successor, and check revocation publication and consumption.\n\nRecover according to state [#recover-according-to-state]\n\n| Situation                                 | Procedure                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Valid certificate, outside the window     | Schedule the request within the window. Do not use `simpleenroll` to bypass it.                                                                                                                                                           |\n| Expired certificate, key available        | With certificate authentication, review `enable_expired_renewal` and whether Gateway permits presenting that credential. Revocation, subject and the remaining checks still apply.                                                        |\n| Renewal through webhook or `NO_AUTH`      | The mTLS expiry check does not run in these modes. Define expiry and authorization policy in the corresponding admission process; the webhook must explicitly authorize the recovery it permits.                                          |\n| Lost or inaccessible key                  | You cannot prove possession of the previous identity. Authorize new admission and, if appropriate, controlled replacement; revoke the compromised identity.                                                                               |\n| Current identity revoked                  | `simplereenroll` rejects the revoked current certificate, and retaining its files does not restore a valid connection. Investigate the reason and prepare an authorized readmission procedure; do not disable authentication to force it. |\n| Device without an identity                | Use initial enrollment. For an existing entry, check the replacement restriction in [provisioning](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies).                                                                         |\n| Retired device                            | Review the operational decision before readmission. Decommissioned state and revocation of the entire history are not equivalent.                                                                                                         |\n| Timeout, HTTP 500 or installation failure | Inspect the device, issued certificates and history before retrying. A successor may have been issued or the previous identity revoked.                                                                                                   |\n\n/serverkeygen [#serverkeygen] [#serverkeygen]\n\n`POST /serverkeygen` generates a software key in Lamassu, rebuilds the CSR with that key and performs initial enrollment. The client receives the private key and certificate.\n\nConfiguration [#serverkeygen-configuration] [#serverkeygen-configuration]\n\n`settings.server_keygen_settings` controls key generation. The rest of the operation applies the [initial enrollment configuration](#enrollment-configuration); before testing with a registered ID, check whether that configuration allows certificate replacement.\n\n| Field                                                                                | Description                                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| <span id=\"enable-key-generation\" />`enabled` (**Enable server-side key generation**) | Turns on the operation. With the operation disabled, the console omits the `key` field.                                                                         |\n| <span id=\"key-type\" />`key.type` (**Key type**)                                      | RSA or ECDSA. The issuance profile must allow the selected algorithm and size.                                                                                  |\n| <span id=\"key-size\" />`key.bits`                                                     | Modulus size for RSA or curve for ECDSA. The console offers 2048, 3072 or 4096 bits for RSA (default 4096) and P-256, P-384 or P-521 for ECDSA (default P-256). |\n\nFor ECDSA, the service also recognizes 224, 256, 384 and 521 as P-224, P-256, P-384 and P-521, and an unrecognized value selects P-256. Choose a compatible size and check the resulting key.\n\nRequest and response [#serverkeygen-request-and-response] [#serverkeygen-request-and-response]\n\nSend a Base64-encoded DER PKCS#10 CSR with `Content-Type: application/pkcs10`. The response uses this operation’s multipart format even if `Accept` asks for PEM. Lamassu generates the key in software even when the CA signs with an HSM-backed key.\n\n<span id=\"response-format\" />\n\nThe body is **MIME multipart/mixed**, with the boundary specified in `Content-Type`, and contains two parts:\n\n| Part                                            | Content after decoding its Base64             |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `application/pkcs8`                             | Unencrypted DER PKCS#8 private key.           |\n| `application/pkcs7-mime; smime-type=certs-only` | DER PKCS#7 containing the issued certificate. |\n\nThe controller also adds `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64` to the outer header, although the outer body is MIME text. **Do not Base64-decode the entire body.** Read the actual boundary and decode each part according to its headers.\n\nExample [#request-and-extraction] [#request-and-extraction]\n\nThe example uses `EST_BASE` and the trust and bootstrap files from [Before you begin](#before-you-begin). Choose an available and admitted ID, such as `est-serverkeygen-001`. The `server-request.key` only signs the input CSR; the received identity uses the server-generated key.\n\n```bash\ntest ! -e server-request.key\nopenssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out server-request.key\nopenssl req -new -sha256 -key server-request.key \\\n  -subj '/CN=est-serverkeygen-001' -out serverkeygen.csr\nopenssl req -in serverkeygen.csr -outform DER -out serverkeygen.csr.der\nopenssl base64 -A -in serverkeygen.csr.der -out serverkeygen.csr.b64\ncurl --fail-with-body --show-error --silent --max-time 30 \\\n  --cacert est-trust.pem --cert bootstrap.crt --key bootstrap.key \\\n  --header 'Content-Type: application/pkcs10' \\\n  --data-binary @serverkeygen.csr.b64 \\\n  --dump-header serverkeygen.headers --output serverkeygen.mime \\\n  \"$EST_BASE/serverkeygen\"\n```\n\n<span id=\"extraction-and-verification\" />\n\nProcess the MIME parts according to their headers and verify that the received key matches the certificate. Inspect the CN, serial, dates and uses, and check the association in Lamassu before installing. Protect files containing the key and remove temporary copies according to the device storage mechanism.\n\n<Cards>\n  <Card title=\"Authentication and trust\" description=\"Bootstrap credentials, the webhook contract and revocation checks.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/bootstrap-identity\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Devices and identities\" description=\"Check the slot, history and status of each issued identity.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/device-management\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Enrollment troubleshooting\" description=\"Locate transport, authentication, registration, issuance and installation failures.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/est/troubleshooting\" />\n</Cards>\n"]
